\title{Average number of HI ionizations per primary particle\\
\large Intergalactic medium at $z=10$, $x_e = 10^{-4}$\\
\large IC secondary photoionization, with an energy-dependent $W(K_e)$}
\author{Generated with an AI assistant, under the project Master Rules}
\date{Reproduced by \texttt{python3 ionization\_yield.py}}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We compute the mean number of \ion{H}{i} ionizations produced by one primary
electron ($10^{2}$--$10^{12}$~eV) or photon ($10^{1}$--$10^{5}$~eV) in the
neutral intergalactic medium at $z=10$ with a static ionized fraction
$x_e = 10^{-4}$, counting every ionization anywhere in the cascade. The mean
energy per ion pair is treated as a genuine function of energy,
$W(K_e) = E_{\rm th}/f_{\rm ion}(K_e,x_e)$, taken from Furlanetto \&
Stoever~\cite{fs10} eq.~(13): identically zero below \SI{28}{\electronvolt},
\SI{43.5}{\electronvolt} at \SI{100}{\electronvolt}, and
\SI{36.5}{\electronvolt} asymptotically. Above
$E_{\rm crit} = \SI{1.303e5}{\electronvolt}$\src{E\_crit\_eV} the
\emph{collisional} yield saturates at
$8.869\times10^{3}$\src{N\_e\_ic\_1e12} ion pairs, because at $z=10$ the CMB
energy density is $14641$ times its present value and inverse-Compton (IC)
scattering carries the electron's energy away. That energy is not lost: between
roughly $10^{7}$ and $10^{9}$~eV the upscattered CMB photons clear the
\SI{13.6}{\electronvolt} threshold while staying below the escape energy
\SI{1218}{\electronvolt}\src{E\_escape\_eV}, and they photoionize. Following
them raises the saturated yield to
$4.575\times10^{6}$\src{N\_e\_C\_1e+12} ion pairs, a factor
$515.9$\src{N\_e\_C\_over\_B\_max}. Replacing the previously used constant
$W = \SI{36.26}{\electronvolt}$ by $W(K_e)$ lowers that headline number by
$0.0956$\src{ic\_W\_energy\_dependence\_reduction}, because the IC secondaries
deposit near threshold where $W$ is largest. Two one-sided modelling brackets
remain and no statistical error bar exists: the photon-transport prescription
moves the answer by $0.0516$\src{ic\_transport\_spread\_max}, and
$0.2297$\src{ic\_frac\_of\_secondary\_below\_100eV} of the secondary yield is
made below \SI{100}{\electronvolt}, where \cite{fs10} caution that the fit is
poor.
\end{abstract}

% ===========================================================================
\section{Assumptions}
% ===========================================================================
\begin{enumerate}\itemsep2pt
\item \textbf{Target.} Pure atomic hydrogen at the mean cosmic density; helium
      omitted from the target. \emph{Inconsistent} with assumption~4, whose fits
      are for a primordial H\,+\,He gas; the inconsistency is of order the
      \ion{He}{i} channel, $f_{\rm ion,HeI} = 0.05409$\src{f\_ion\_HeI}.
\item \textbf{Ionized fraction} static at $x_e = 10^{-4}$.
\item \textbf{Uniform gas}, no clumping.
\item \textbf{Energy partition} from Shull \& van Steenberg~\cite{svds}, in the
      energy-dependent parametrisation of Ricotti et al.~\cite{rgs02} as
      reproduced in \cite{fs10} eq.~(13). \S\ref{sec:svds} reports what is and
      is not verified about those coefficients.
\item \textbf{$W$ is a function of energy}, $W(K_e) = E_{\rm th}/f_{\rm
      ion}(K_e,x_e)$, and is identically zero (no ionization) below
      \SI{28}{\electronvolt}. \cite{fs10} caution that this fit is ``a
      relatively poor match at lower energies''; that caution is carried through
      to a bracket in \S\ref{sec:W}, not buried.
\item \textbf{Secondary-electron energy distribution} from \cite{fs10} eq.~(2),
      $p(\varepsilon)\propto[1+(\varepsilon/\bar\varepsilon)^{2.1}]^{-1}$ with
      $\bar\varepsilon = \SI{8}{\electronvolt}$ for \ion{H}{i}. Used for the
      independent bound of \S\ref{sec:cascade}, and anchored on the median
      \cite{fs10} quote for it.
\item \textbf{``Per primary''} means per particle that \emph{does} interact. The
      primary is not transported cosmologically; the secondary IC photons are,
      through assumption~10.
\item \textbf{No magnetic field}; the CMB dominates at $z=10$ regardless.
\item \textbf{Flat $\Lambda$CDM}, Planck 2018~\cite{planck}. \textbf{Thomson-regime
      IC with isotropic seeds}, spectrum from Blumenthal \& Gould~\cite{bg70};
      the Thomson parameter is
      $9.894\times10^{-3}$\src{thomson\_parameter\_at\_Emax} at the top of the
      range, so the approximation holds throughout and fails above it.
\item \textbf{Absorption prescription for the secondaries.} Absorbed with
      probability $1-e^{-\tau}$, $\tau = n_{\rm HI}\sigma_{\rm pi}c/H(z)$:
      absorbed near $z=10$, or never. \emph{A choice}; the light-cone
      alternative is computed and differs by
      $0.0516$\src{ic\_transport\_spread\_max}.
\item \textbf{One generation} of secondary photons --- \emph{derived}, and
      exactly, in \S\ref{sec:closure}.
\end{enumerate}

% ===========================================================================
\section{Derivation}
% ===========================================================================

\subsection{Background at $z=10$}
With Planck 2018 parameters ($\Omega_b h^2 = 0.02242$, $h=0.6766$,
$\Omega_m = 0.3111$), $T_{\rm CMB,0}=\SI{2.7255}{\kelvin}$, $X = 1-Y_p = 0.755$:
\begin{align}
n_{\rm H}(z) &= \frac{\Omega_b\rho_{\rm crit,0}X}{m_{\rm H}}(1+z)^3
   = \SI{2.529e-4}{\per\cubic\centi\metre}\src{n\_H\_cm3},\\
u_{\rm CMB}(z) &= a_{\rm rad}T_{\rm CMB,0}^4(1+z)^4
   = \SI{6.112e-9}{\erg\per\cubic\centi\metre}\src{u\_cmb\_erg\_cm3},\qquad
kT_{\rm CMB} = \SI{2.584e-3}{\electronvolt}\src{kT\_cmb\_eV}.
\end{align}

\subsection{An energy-dependent $W$, and what is known about its
            coefficients}\label{sec:svds}
Shull \& van Steenberg~\cite{svds} ran a Monte Carlo degradation of a fast
electron in a primordial gas and fitted the fraction of its \emph{initial}
energy reaching each channel as a function of the ionized fraction $x$. Because
the fit reports the fraction of the initial energy, it already sums over
\emph{every} generation of the cascade the electron starts --- the secondaries it
ejects, their secondaries, and so on down to the sub-excitation electrons. That
is why the yield can be written in one line at all. Note also \cite{fs10}
sec.~6: ``our parameters do not include the effects of the initial ionization
event that generates the electron'', which is exactly why $N_\gamma$ below
carries its own explicit $+1$.

The asymptotic form has no energy dependence. Ricotti et al.~\cite{rgs02} fitted
the energy dependence as well, and \cite{fs10} reproduce it as their eq.~(13):
\begin{equation}
f_{\rm ion,HI}(E,x) =
  -0.69\left(\frac{\SI{28}{\electronvolt}}{E}\right)^{0.4}
   x^{0.2}\left(1-x^{0.38}\right)^{2}
  + 0.39\left(1-x^{0.41}\right)^{1.76},\qquad E>\SI{28}{\electronvolt},
\label{eq:rgs}
\end{equation}
and zero otherwise, with a companion expression for $f_{\rm heat}$ (their
eq.~14) of a \emph{different} functional form, $A[1-(1-x^{B})^{C}]$ rather than
$A(1-x^{B})^{C}$. Hence
\begin{equation}
W(K_e) \equiv \frac{E_{\rm th}}{f_{\rm ion,HI}(K_e,x_e)}
 = \infty,\ \SI{43.5}{\electronvolt},\ \SI{38.9}{\electronvolt},\
   \SI{36.5}{\electronvolt}
\quad\text{at } K_e < 28,\ 10^{2},\ 10^{3},\ 10^{6}~\text{eV}.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{The coefficients are now checked.} Until \cite{fs10} entered
\texttt{papers/} the twelve recalled Shull \& van Steenberg coefficients had no
external test at all. Eq.~\eqref{eq:rgs} carries the asymptote at two
significant figures, and the comparison is:
\begin{itemize}\itemsep1pt
\item ionization: recalled $0.3908(1-x^{0.4092})^{1.7592} = 0.3751$ against
      eq.~\eqref{eq:rgs}'s $0.39(1-x^{0.41})^{1.76}$, relative difference
      $0.001764$\src{svds\_vs\_fs10\_ion\_reldiff};
\item heating: recalled $0.9971[1-(1-x^{0.2663})^{1.3163}]$ against
      \cite{fs10} eq.~(14), relative difference
      $0.01101$\src{svds\_vs\_fs10\_heat\_reldiff}.
\end{itemize}
\cite{fs10} footnote~8 states a further hard number: the SvdS \emph{exact}
heating fraction at $x_i = 0.01$ is $0.32$, and the Ricotti \emph{fit} sits
$\sim6$ per cent above it in absolute terms. The recalled coefficients give
$0.3658$\src{svds\_heat\_x0.01}, i.e.\ $4.6$ per cent absolute above $0.32$ ---
so they reproduce the \emph{fitting function}, which is what they are, and
inherit its known offset from the underlying Monte Carlo. That offset is a real
systematic and is \emph{not} removed here.

\paragraph{What is still not available.} \cite{fs10}'s own results are better
than the fit used here, and they are not usable: ``Electronic tables of our
results are available on request'' (abstract), and sec.~7, ``we have not
attempted to find another form. Instead, we recommend interpolating the exact
results.'' There is no published FS10 fitting formula and no public table.
Reconstructing one from memory is the failure mode this project exists to
prevent, so it was not done.

\subsection{Models (A) and (B)}
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;N_{\rm ion}^{(A)}(E) = f_{\rm ion}(E,x_e)\,\frac{E}{E_{\rm th}}\;}
\label{eq:modelA}
\end{equation}
The electron loses energy to the gas at the Berger--Seltzer collision stopping
power~\cite{icru37}, nearly energy-independent in the relativistic regime, and
to the CMB at $b_{\rm IC} = \frac43\sigma_{\rm T}c\,u_{\rm CMB}\gamma^2\beta^2$
\cite{rl}, which grows as $\gamma^2$; the two are equal at
$E_{\rm crit} = \SI{1.303e5}{\electronvolt}$\src{E\_crit\_eV}. Counting only the
collisional branch,
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;N_{\rm ion}^{(B)}(E) = \int_0^{E}
   \frac{b_{\rm coll}(T)}{b_{\rm coll}(T)+b_{\rm IC}(T)}\,
   \frac{dN^{(A)}}{dT}\,dT \;}
\label{eq:modelB}
\end{equation}
The integrand is the \emph{marginal} yield $dN^{(A)}/dT$, not $f_{\rm
ion}/E_{\rm th}$: once $f_{\rm ion}$ depends on energy the two differ, and only
the former is the number of ion pairs bought by the energy deposited between $T$
and $T+dT$. With a constant $f_{\rm ion}$ it collapses to the old expression
exactly, which is a check rather than a remark. The integral converges, so the
collisional yield \emph{saturates}: the gas ever receives only
$\SI{3.238e5}{\electronvolt}$\src{E\_dep\_eV\_1e12} of an arbitrarily energetic
electron, giving $8.869\times10^{3}$\src{N\_e\_ic\_1e12} ion pairs. Model~(B)
discards the IC branch and is a lower bound.

\subsection{The IC-secondary channel}\label{sec:secondary}
An electron of Lorentz factor $\gamma$ upscatters a CMB photon of energy
$\varepsilon_0$ to $\langle\varepsilon_1\rangle =
\frac43\gamma^2\varepsilon_0$. For isotropic monochromatic seeds in the Thomson
limit the scattered spectrum is~\cite{bg70}
$dN/dx = 3F(x)$, $F(x)=2x\ln x + x + 1 - 2x^2$, $x=\varepsilon_1/4\gamma^2
\varepsilon_0\in[0,1]$; we compute rather than assert $\int_0^1 3F\,dx = 1$ and
$\int_0^1 3Fx\,dx = \frac13$, the second of which \emph{is} the mean-energy
relation. Convolving with a Planck seed and writing
$w=\varepsilon_1/(4\gamma^2kT)$,
\begin{equation}
\tilde p(w) = 3\int_{\ln w}^{\infty}\!\!\hat f(e^v)F(we^{-v})\,dv,\qquad
\int\tilde p\,dw = 1,\qquad
\langle w\rangle = \frac{\langle u\rangle}{3} = 0.9004\src{ic\_w\_mean},
\label{eq:ptilde}
\end{equation}
which is \emph{universal} --- independent of $\gamma$. Only the map
$\varepsilon_1 = 4\gamma^2kT\,w$ carries $\gamma$, and that is what makes the
problem semi-analytic. Setting $\langle\varepsilon_1\rangle = E_{\rm th}$ gives
$\gamma\approx38$, $T_e = \SI{1.902e7}{\electronvolt}$\src{ic\_turnon\_T\_eV\_pred}
--- written down before the grid was evaluated, which then placed the
half-maximum at $\SI{1.149e7}{\electronvolt}$\src{ic\_turnon\_T\_eV\_half}.
Absorption requires $\tau>1$, i.e.\ $\varepsilon_1$ below
$E_{\rm esc} = \SI{1218}{\electronvolt}$\src{E\_escape\_eV}. Collecting,
\begin{equation}
G(\gamma) = \int_0^\infty \tilde q(w)\,
   \frac{\left[1-e^{-\tau(\varepsilon_1)}\right]N_\gamma(\varepsilon_1)}
        {\varepsilon_1}\,dw,\qquad
   \tilde q(w) \equiv \frac{w\,\tilde p(w)}{\langle w\rangle},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\;N_{\rm ion}^{(C)}(E) = N_{\rm ion}^{(B)}(E)
  + \int_0^{E}\frac{b_{\rm IC}(T)}{b_{\rm coll}(T)+b_{\rm IC}(T)}\,
    G(\gamma(T))\,dT\;}
\label{eq:modelC}
\end{equation}
$\tilde q$ is the \emph{energy}-weighted spectrum, so (B) and (C) share one
branching function and cannot disagree about where the energy went; this is
verified to $4\times10^{-16}$.

\subsection{An independent bound from the secondary-electron
            spectrum}\label{sec:cascade}
Everything above rests on the Shull \& van Steenberg branching fractions.
\cite{fs10} eq.~(2) offers a route that uses none of them. If \emph{every}
inelastic event were an ionization, the yield would obey
\begin{equation}
Y(E) = 1 + \big\langle\,Y(\varepsilon) + Y(E-E_{\rm th}-\varepsilon)\,
        \big\rangle_{p(\varepsilon)},\qquad Y(E<E_{\rm th}) = 0,
\label{eq:cascade}
\end{equation}
with $p$ from eq.~(2) and $\varepsilon<(E-E_{\rm th})/2$ by \cite{fs10}'s own
definition of which outgoing electron is the ``secondary''. Switching off
excitation and heating can only \emph{add} ionizations, so $Y$ is a strict upper
bound --- and a far tighter one than $E/E_{\rm th}$. It gives one ion pair per
$\SI{17.93}{\electronvolt}$\src{W\_ion\_only\_eV}, against
$W\approx\SI{36}{\electronvolt}$ in reality; the factor of two is the energy
that genuinely goes to excitation and heat, and it is consistent with
\cite{fs10}'s finding that ionization, heating and excitation split roughly
equally at high energy. Model~(A) stays under $Y$ at every energy tested. Two
prescriptions from the same paper, used in completely different ways, do not
contradict each other.

\subsection{Why one generation suffices}\label{sec:closure}
Exactly, not approximately. An absorbed photon has
$\varepsilon_1\lesssim E_{\rm esc}$, so its photoelectron has $\gamma = 1.0024$;
for \emph{that} electron to upscatter a CMB photon back over
\SI{13.6}{\electronvolt} needs a seed of
$E_{\rm th}/4\gamma^2 = \SI{3.383}{\electronvolt} = 1310\,kT$, Planck occupancy
$e^{-1310}\sim10^{-569}$. On the tracked spectrum $G(\gamma)=0$ identically.
Nor can a pair cascade start: the hardest photon tracked is
$2.4\times10^{12}$~eV against a $\gamma\gamma$ threshold on the CMB of
$\SI{3.742e13}{\electronvolt}$\src{eps\_pairprod\_threshold\_eV}.

\subsection{Photons}
$N_\gamma\equiv0$ below $E_{\rm th}$, exactly. Above it,
$N_\gamma = 1 + N_e(E_\gamma - E_{\rm th})$. Since $f_{\rm ion}$ vanishes below
\SI{28}{\electronvolt}, a second exact statement follows: $N_\gamma\equiv1$ all
the way to $E_{\rm th}+\SI{28}{\electronvolt} = \SI{41.6}{\electronvolt}$ ---
one ionization, no more, no rounding. Over the requested photon range
($\le10^5$~eV) the photoelectron has $\gamma\le1.2$ and upscatters nothing, so
models (B) and (C) coincide for photons: \emph{the IC channel does not change
the photon answer at all.}

