$10^{12}$ & $2.75\times10^{10}$ & $8.87\times10^{3}$ & $4.58\times10^{6}$\src{N\_e\_C\_1e+12}  & ---                & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Headline numbers, with units.}
\begin{itemize}\itemsep1pt
\item $W(K_e)$: $\infty$ below \SI{28}{\electronvolt}, \SI{43.5}{\electronvolt} at
      \SI{100}{\electronvolt}, \SI{38.9}{\electronvolt} at \SI{1}{\kilo\electronvolt},
      \SI{36.5}{\electronvolt} asymptotically. The asymptote matches the measured
      $W(\mathrm{H})\approx\SI{36}{\electronvolt}$.
\item $E_{\rm crit} = \SI{1.303e5}{\electronvolt}$\src{E\_crit\_eV};
      $E_{\rm esc} = \SI{1218}{\electronvolt}$\src{E\_escape\_eV}.
\item $N_{\rm max}^{(C)} = 4.575\times10^{6}$\src{N\_e\_C\_1e+12} ion pairs for
      \emph{any} electron above $\sim\SI{1}{\giga\electronvolt}$, a factor
      $515.9$\src{N\_e\_C\_over\_B\_max} above the collisional-only saturation.
\item Peak reprocessing efficiency
      $0.9897$\src{ic\_max\_energy\_fraction\_absorbed}.
\item Independent ceiling from \cite{fs10} eq.~(2):
      one pair per $\SI{17.93}{\electronvolt}$\src{W\_ion\_only\_eV}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Correction: model (A) \emph{is} an upper bound after
            all}\label{sec:notbound}
The previous revision of this calculation reported that model~(C) exceeds
model~(A) by a few per cent near $10^{8}$~eV, and explained it: a photon just
above threshold buys an ion pair for \SI{13.6}{\electronvolt} where electron
degradation needs $W$. That explanation was right about the mechanism and wrong
about the size, because it used the \emph{asymptotic} $W$ for the
near-threshold secondaries that do most of the work. With $W(K_e)$ those
secondaries are correctly charged \SI{43.5}{\electronvolt} rather than
\SI{36.26}{\electronvolt} per pair, and the excess disappears:
$\max(N^{(C)}/N^{(A)}) = 1.000$\src{N\_e\_C\_over\_A\_max}. \textbf{The claim
that (A) is not an upper bound was an artefact of the constant-$W$
approximation and is withdrawn.} The mechanism survives; its magnitude did not.

\subsection{What $W(K_e)$ changed, and what it did not}\label{sec:W}
Replacing the constant $W$ costs
$0.0956$\src{ic\_W\_energy\_dependence\_reduction} of the headline number. It
matters here and almost nowhere else, because the IC channel deposits through
photons of a few tens to a few hundred eV --- precisely where $W(K_e)$ departs
from its asymptote.

It does \emph{not} close the low-energy bracket.
$0.2297$\src{ic\_frac\_of\_secondary\_below\_100eV} of the secondary yield is
still produced below \SI{100}{\electronvolt}, and \cite{fs10} state plainly that
eq.~\eqref{eq:rgs} is ``a relatively poor match at lower energies''. Suppressing
the photoelectron cascade entirely below \SI{100}{\electronvolt} --- the
pessimistic extreme --- gives
$4.244\times10^{6}$\src{N\_e\_C\_1e12\_pe\_floor\_100eV}, i.e.\ $-7.2\%$. The
truth is inside that bracket. Closing it requires \cite{fs10}'s tables, which
are not public.

\paragraph{Uncertainty.} \textbf{No statistical interval is quoted, and that is
a limitation rather than a claim of precision.} No input carries a covariance.
Three variations \emph{are} propagated: the mean excitation energy $I$ moves the
collisional saturation by $1.52\%$; the transport prescription moves $N^{(C)}$
by $0.0516$\src{ic\_transport\_spread\_max}; the sub-\SI{100}{\electronvolt}
bracket moves it by $-7.2\%$. All three are one-sided or asymmetric and the
modelling choices of \S\ref{sec:notchecked} dominate.

% ===========================================================================
\section{What was \emph{not} checked}\label{sec:notchecked}
% ===========================================================================
\begin{enumerate}\itemsep2pt
\item \textbf{The Shull \& van Steenberg paper itself is still not in the tree.}
      The coefficients are now checked against \cite{fs10}'s reproduction of the
      Ricotti fit to two significant figures, which is a real external test, but
      it is a test against a \emph{fit to} SvdS, not against SvdS. \cite{fs10}
      quantify the gap: $\sim6\%$ absolute in $f_{\rm heat}$ at $x_i=0.01$.
\item \textbf{\cite{fs10}'s own tables} are not public and are not used. They
      would supersede eq.~\eqref{eq:rgs} entirely and are the single largest
      available improvement.
\item \textbf{Which transport prescription is right} is not settled; only the
      spread is measured.
\item \textbf{Compton scattering of the secondaries} off bound electrons is
      omitted ($\tau_{\rm T}\sim0.1$ over a Hubble length); it would slightly
      \emph{raise} model~(C).
\item \textbf{Helium} is in the fits but not the target, and \ion{He}{i} opacity
      is absent from $\tau$.
\item \textbf{Klein--Nishina} suppression above $10^{12}$~eV would raise (B) and
      lower (C).
\item \textbf{The ionization-only cascade neglects} excitation and
      electron-electron heating by construction. That is what makes it a bound;
      it is not a model, and it is not used as one.
\item \textbf{No independent reimplementation} (check C24). Every cross-check
      except the $W$ anchor, the \cite{fs10} comparisons and the eq.~(2) bound
      is internal to this one code.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Reproducibility.} Deterministic; no random numbers, so no seed. Run
\texttt{python3 ionization\_yield.py} to regenerate every number, the registry
(\texttt{results.json}), the provenance records and both figure formats; then
\texttt{python3 check\_provenance.py} to refuse the build if paper and registry
have drifted. Environment: \texttt{numpy}~1.26.4, \texttt{scipy}~1.11.4,
\texttt{matplotlib}~3.8.0, Python~3.11.

